          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"alcott-little-women-9ca93320ba262179\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":1,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.96,\"analysis\":\"Meg’s embedded affirmation that she does not love her mother any less primarily avows the continuing strength of her filial love. It has a weak commitment-like resonance because it reassures her mother that marriage will not displace their bond, but the core wording does not substantially undertake an obligation. The emotional delivery does not independently establish expressive/reflexive force.\",\"ambiguity\":\"There is slight T/P ambiguity: the avowal helps affirm relational continuity, but any undertaking is conveyed mainly by the surrounding promises to visit and remain involved rather than by the love clause itself.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Meg directly addresses her mother at her wedding farewell; the equivalent of “I love you” is grammatically embedded in “Don’t feel … that I love you any the less.”\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"At the transition from her childhood home to married life, Meg reassures Marmee that loving John and leaving with him do not diminish her filial love. Her clinging and tearful eyes indicate tenderness and intensity, while her stated plans to visit and preserve her place in the family reinforce continuity; these contextual features do not turn the core avowal into a reflex-like discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Don’t feel that I am separated from you, Marmee dear, or that I love you any the less for loving John so much”\",\"supports\":\"The contrast explicitly presents Meg’s continuing love for her mother as true, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Meg speaks while “clinging to her mother, with full eyes for a moment.”\",\"supports\":\"This establishes affectionate, emotional delivery but supplies no independent evidence that the words are an expressive/reflex-like discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“I shall come every day, Father, and expect to keep my old place in all your hearts, though I am married.”\",\"supports\":\"The surrounding farewell frames the avowal as reassurance about continued family attachment and provides limited contextual support for commitment-like force.\",\"confidence\":0.94},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is located in Chapter Twenty-Five of Louisa May Alcott’s Little Women and is marked as an affirmative present-form explicit love expression.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms the identified occurrence and its affirmative present-state form.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T straightforwardly captures the core avowal, with slight P support for relational reassurance. Emotional farewell details remain contextual, so neither E nor O is needed.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
      ],
